    <!--
    Rust workspace source set.  Built with Cargo, not by this manifest.

    The fifteen C translation units above remain the authoritative description of
    the retained reference implementation, and they are what this manifest builds.
    They are read and never edited: they are the algorithmic oracle that the
    memory-safe Rust reimplementation of this library is developed and
    differentially tested against.  The Rust crates below carry no source, depend
    or include-file entry of their own, deliberately: a .rs path offered to a
    consumer of this manifest would be offered to a C compiler.

    Workspace root:

      Cargo.toml           virtual manifest: members, shared package metadata,
                           workspace lints, release profile
      Cargo.lock           committed, because this workspace ships a system library
      rust-toolchain.toml  toolchain channel, with rustfmt and clippy
      .rustfmt.toml        formatting policy
      clippy.toml          lint thresholds
      deny.toml            cargo deny license and advisory policy
      cbindgen.toml        C header generation.  Specifies the eleven-rule
                           comparison against zlib.h, including rule A11's
                           literal signature diff, which must be empty

    crates/zlib-rs         The safe core.  Carries forbid(unsafe_code) with no_std
                           plus alloc and an empty dependencies table, so it pulls
                           in no third party crate at all.  Every algorithm lives
                           here.
                           subtrees:   adler32/  crc32/  deflate/  trees/
                                       inflate/ (inftrees and inffast folded in)
                                       gz/
                           top level:  lib.rs  allocate.rs  error.rs  config.rs
                                       read_buf.rs  weak_slice.rs  infback.rs
                                       compress.rs  uncompress.rs

    crates/libz-rs-sys     The C ABI facade: the only crate that exports symbols and
                           the only shipped crate that writes unsafe, under
                           deny(unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn) with an invariant named at
                           every block.  The safe wrappers that let the differential
                           suites, the benchmarks and the fuzz targets drive this C
                           ABI without raw pointers of their own are NOT here: they
                           are the boundary gates in the dev-only
                           crates/zlib-rs-differential/src/port.rs, kept outside the
                           facade because AAP 0.6.1 fixes the facade's unsafe at the
                           six unavoidable C-ABI site categories and a test harness
                           is not one of them.  The one other place that declares raw
                           entry points is the dev-only
                           crates/zlib-rs-differential/src/oracle.rs, which is where
                           the C oracle is declared.  [lib] name = "z" with
                           crate-type = ["cdylib", "staticlib", "rlib"] makes Cargo
                           emit libz.so and libz.a from one definition of the
                           exported surface.  csrc/ holds TWO C translation units
                           and exactly ONE of them ships: gzprintf_shim.c, which
                           defines the variadic gzprintf and the va_list-taking
                           gzvprintf, is archived into the libz.a rustc emits,
                           because stable Rust can declare neither form.  The
                           other, gzvprintf_probe.c, builds a va_list and forwards
                           it to gzvprintf, which no Rust caller can do; it reaches
                           TEST TARGETS ONLY, through rustc-link-arg-tests, and is
                           never part of the shipped library.  There is no
                           inftrees_shim.c: an earlier revision of this file
                           described one, presenting inftrees.h's codetype
                           prototype, and it was removed once inflate_table was
                           defined in Rust over a validated plain int -- an
                           enumerator of 0..2 is passed exactly as an int, so no C
                           was needed for it.
                           build.rs handles what belongs to the build: it emits the
                           link arguments, publishes one cfg, compiles those two
                           shipped C units plus the test-only probe, and prunes
                           stale artifact aliases.  Packaging is
                           NOT its work and cannot be: a build script runs before
                           the link, so a versioned name it created would dangle.
                           The relink through zlib.map, the SONAME and the symlink
                           chain belong to Makefile.in's rust target and to the
                           equivalent block in CMakeLists.txt.

    crates/zlib-rs-differential
                           Dev-only oracle harness.  Its build script compiles the
                           C units above into a test-only archive, so both
                           implementations are co-resident in one test process and
                           comparing them is a memory compare.  It appears in the
                           dependencies table of neither shipped crate, which is
                           what keeps reference zlib an oracle rather than a build
                           dependency.  Driving two C ABIs does mean it needs
                           unsafe of its own; that is confined to src/oracle.rs
                           and src/port.rs, and every suite under tests/, all
                           five fuzz targets, and the three criterion suites of
                           the excluded benches package that depends on this crate
                           by path, all carry forbid(unsafe_code).

    fuzz/                  Dev-only cargo fuzz targets; its own package and its own
                           workspace, excluded from the root one.
    benches/               Dev-only criterion benchmark suites: the package
                           zlib-rs-benches, with its own Cargo.toml, its own
                           [workspace] table and its own Cargo.lock, excluded from
                           the root workspace because criterion's MSRV is higher
                           than the library's.  It depends on all three crates
                           above by path, so the benches reach the port and the C
                           oracle as an ordinary consumer would; nothing is
                           attached to zlib-rs-differential by a bench entry.  Run
                           them with cargo bench and manifest-path benches.

    Packaging is unaffected on the ELF platforms where the Rust drop-in is staged
    at all, which is where a linker accepts soname and version-script and is
    verified on x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.  There, both implementations emit the
    same library named by dlname above, install the same two public headers, and
    export the same ABI under the same zlib.map version script: the real file
    libz.so.1.3.2.1-motley with SONAME libz.so.1, two symlinks (libz.so.1 and
    libz.so) pointing DIRECTLY at that file rather than through one another, and
    libz.a.  A consumer includes zlib.h and dynamically links -lz either way,
    unrecompiled.  Static linking is equivalent too, and is the one case that
    needed something added rather than merely reproduced: a Rust libz.a carries the
    Rust runtime, whose companion libraries an archive cannot record for itself, so
    the target's set is probed once from rustc and published as the single
    Libs.private key.  make rust-pc writes that descriptor beside the staged
    library, asserting every other field is the C one byte for byte, and then
    compiles, links and runs a C consumer from nothing but the flags pkg-config
    answers; the CMake path substitutes the same key into zlib.pc and sets it on
    the exported ZLIB::ZLIBSTATIC target as well.  zlib.pc.in states the reasoning.

    Entry points, spelled without the leading dashes their cargo and make options
    need, because an XML comment may not hold two consecutive hyphens.  README and
    Makefile.in carry the exact command lines, and the workflow that runs every one
    of them is .github/workflows/rust.yml:

      cargo build      release profile, packages zlib-rs and libz-rs-sys
      cargo build      release profile, package libz-rs-sys, features libz-compat
      cargo test       release profile, whole workspace
      make rust        stage the installable shared and static drop-in libraries
      make rust-pc     emit the pkg-config descriptor for the staged library and
                       gate it by linking and running a C consumer from nothing
                       but the flags pkg-config answers
      make rust-test   link and run the unmodified C drivers against them
      make rust-header regenerate the cbindgen header, compile it standalone in
                       four dialects, and compare the function names it declares
                       against the ones zlib.h declares, in both directions.
                       Comparing SHAPE, meaning signatures, struct layouts and
                       macro values, and diffing a compiler resolved rendering of
                       every contract signature with an empty diff required,
                       belongs to the header job of
                       .github/workflows/rust.yml, which invokes this target first
      make rust-symbols diff the staged library's exported symbols against a built
                       C library; unlike make rust it fails rather than skips when
                       that library is absent
      make rust-msrv   type check the root workspace on the declared 1.80 floor
                       with warnings denied: cargo metadata locked, then cargo
                       check over the workspace and again over the two shipped
                       crates with all features.  It does not build artifacts and
                       does not run tests, and the excluded benches and fuzz
                       workspaces are outside it: benches declares a higher floor
                       of its own and fuzz needs nightly
      make rust-clean  remove the Cargo target directory and the staged artifacts
      cmake            configure with the ZLIB_BUILD_RUST option for the same
                       drop-in through CMake
    -->
</package>

